# User Sets

A **user set** describes a group of users by composition rather than by listing them. Instead of naming five people, a set can say "anyone with the admin role" or "anyone on this list, plus these two people" — and it re-resolves every time it is used, so it stays correct as membership changes.

Sets appear wherever something needs to be restricted to a smaller group than the whole collective. On a [decision](/help/decisions), two independent sets control who can add options and who can vote.

## Syntax

A set is written as a list of **clauses** separated by spaces. A user belongs to the set if **any** clause matches, so each clause only ever widens it.

```
user:@alice,@bob role:admin list:a1b2c3d4
```

That set contains Alice, Bob, everyone holding the `admin` role, and everyone on list `a1b2c3d4`.

An **empty** set means no restriction — everyone who can already reach the thing may act on it. There is no clause for "everyone": that is the absence of a restriction, not a group of users.

## Clauses

| Clause | Matches |
|--------|---------|
| `user:<handle>` | The named users. Accepts several, comma-separated: `user:@alice,@bob`. The `@` is optional. |
| `role:<name>` | Everyone holding that role in the collective: `admin`, `representative`, `summarizer`, `automator`, `moderator`. |
| `list:<id>` | Everyone on that [list](/help/lists), identified by its 8-character id. The list must be public. |
| `members` | Every member of the collective. Must stand alone — every other clause already selects members, so combining them changes nothing. |

The syntax follows the same conventions as [search filters](/help/search): `key:value`, space-separated, comma-separated values, and an optional `@` on handles.

## Rules

**Handles are resolved when the set is saved.** A stored set holds user ids, so it keeps working after someone changes their handle.

**Named users must be members of the collective.** A set cannot grant access to someone outside it — it narrows an existing audience, never widens it.

**Lists must be public.** A private list is visible only to its owner, but the things a set governs are not: a decision publishes who voted. Using a private list would expose its membership, so it is refused.

**A broken reference matches nobody.** If a list is deleted, or turned private, or a named user leaves the collective, that clause stops matching while the other clauses keep working. The set narrows rather than silently widening.

**Sets are visible.** Anyone who can read a decision can see the sets that govern it. A restriction you cannot see is one you cannot question.

## Examples

| Set | Meaning |
|-----|---------|
| *(empty)* | Everyone with access |
| `members` | Every member of the collective |
| `role:admin` | Collective admins |
| `user:@dana` | One person |
| `list:a1b2c3d4` | Everyone on that list |
| `user:@alice,@bob role:admin` | Alice, Bob, and every admin |

## Limits

A set may hold up to 10 clauses, and a `user:` clause up to 200 handles.

Sets are unions only. There is currently no way to write an intersection ("admins who are also on this list") or an exclusion ("everyone on this list except Bob").

## Setting a user set

In the web interface, decision settings offer one field per set; leave a field empty for no restriction.

Over [markdown actions](/help/markdown-ui), the [REST API](/help/rest-api), and [MCP](/help/mcp), the same syntax is passed as a string parameter:

```
POST /d/{id}/settings/actions/update_decision_settings
  proposer_eligibility=members
  voter_eligibility=user:@alice,@bob role:admin
```

Sending an empty value clears the restriction. Omitting the parameter leaves the existing set unchanged.
